'' THE MATH FAMILY CAN BE OVERLOADED ON A TYPE, AND THE INTRINSIC MUST STAND ASIDE.
''
'' fbc accepts 'operator sqr( byref x as const TSQR ) as single' and calls it;
'' optimizations/inline-ops.bas declares one and checks the cooked value comes back.
'' tiko took the intrinsic path over the UDT: Coerce refused to make a double of an
'' aggregate and answered NOTHING, and that nothing went out as the operand of an
'' sqrt -- 'emit: ICE: an operand with no kind'.
''
'' FOUND BY NAME, because nothing BINDS it. 'sqr( t )' parses as a CALL and Check types
'' the INTRINSIC; the symbol an 'operator sqr' declares is spelled exactly 'sqr'. ABS
'' is here beside it because it takes a different branch of the same rule -- per-width
'' builtins rather than the shared double one -- and would have gone on declining.
''
'' AND A LIBM NAME THE PROGRAM DECLARES ITSELF. The four libm functions with no LLVM
'' intrinsic are declared by scanning the emitted TEXT for a call, and a call the
'' program wrote looks exactly like one the emitter made: 'declare function asinf
'' alias "asinf"' beside tiko's own ASIN gave two declarations of one name and clang
'' refused the module -- 'invalid redefinition of function asinf'. That is the same
'' corpus file, one error further on.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/user-math-operator.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' THE OPERATORS ARE CALLED.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call float @"_Zv13sqrRK4TSQR"
'' EXPECT-OUT: call float @"_Zv13absRK4TABS"

'' AND NO INTRINSIC IS EMITTED OVER THE AGGREGATE.
'' EXPECT-NOT: llvm.sqrt.f64
'' GOLDEN: expected/user-math-operator.out
